[38;5;231m⏺[39m Approved — Part 1 is locked. Now the testing question I flagged, then I'll write the spec.

  This repo has no test tooling whatsoever: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, no devDependencies, and there's not a single
  test file. So there's nothing to follow here, which makes it your call rather than mine.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the thing actually worth testing is the handler wiring — "a successful login records one event carrying the
  userId the server returned, and nothing else does." That behavior survives the stub being replaced by a real API. But
  it lives inside a DOM event listener, so testing it needs a DOM. The shallower alternatives test values the stub
  invents, which get deleted the day real auth arrives.

  - [1m[38;5;153mnode:test[39m + [38;5;153mjsdom[0m — one real test of the handler wiring. Buys coverage of the durable behavior and a place for
    future tests to go. Costs: the repo's first dependency and a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, in a project that currently has zero.
    Also needs [38;5;153mapp.js[39m to be loadable outside a browser.
  - [1mNo automated tests[0m — verify by opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and reading the console. Buys zero tooling debt in what is
    explicitly a stub. Costs: the wiring is unverified except by eye; regressions are silent. Cheap to reverse — tests
    can be added later, just not for free.
  - [1m[38;5;153mnode:test[39m alone, zero deps[0m — test [38;5;153mlogin[39m's return shape and [38;5;153mtrackLoginEvent[39m via a dual CommonJS/browser export shim.
    Buys a test command with no dependencies. Costs: a [38;5;153mtypeof module !== 'undefined'[39m wart in a 30-line browser file, and
    it asserts made-up stub values rather than real behavior.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153mnode:test[39m + [38;5;153mjsdom[39m.[0m Test infrastructure is cheapest to stand up before the code exists, and it's the
  only option that tests something which will still be true after the stub is replaced. That said — if this codebase is
  a throwaway fixture rather than something you're growing, the second option is genuinely defensible and I won't argue
  with it.
